@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+# Test suite performance notes
+
+These timings are intended for like-for-like comparisons. Use the same RSpec seed and keep PostgreSQL and Redis available. Run one spec directory at a time; do not include system or streaming specs.
+
+## Models phase (2026-11-23)
+
+Baseline command:
+
+```sh
+bundle exec rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 30 --seed 1621
+```
+
+The baseline on this CI-like container contained 1,795 examples, finished with no failures, and took:
+
+- 214.0 seconds of RSpec execution (3:34)
+- 216 seconds wall time
+
+The optimized run uses the same command and seed. It still contains 1,795 examples, finishes with no failures, and takes:
+
+- 135.9 seconds of RSpec execution (2:15.9)
+- 140 seconds wall time
+- 36.5% less RSpec execution time, or 35.2% less wall time (1.57x faster by RSpec time)
+
+### What was measured
+
+TestProf EventProf attributed 66.571 seconds (28.6% of its profiled run) to 3,908 synchronous Sidekiq jobs:
+
+```sh
+EVENT_PROF=sidekiq.inline \
+  EVENT_PROF_TOP=25 \
+  EVENT_PROF_EXAMPLES=1 \
+  FPROF=1 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/models --require test_prof --format progress
+```
+
+The jobs came from model callbacks even though worker execution is covered by the separate worker specs. Model examples now use Sidekiq's fake queue; other spec types retain inline execution. Active Job uses its test adapter only around model examples so mail-capture assertions still execute the intended delivery jobs.
+
+RSpecDissect also showed that repeated setup dominated the optimized suite before fixture sharing (115.344 seconds of setup out of 144.813 seconds):
+
+```sh
+RD_PROF=1 RD_PROF_TOP=20 RD_PROF_LET_TOP=8 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/models --require test_prof --format progress --seed 1621
+```
+
+The largest setup groups included:
+
+| Group | Repeated setup | Examples |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy | 11.234s | 36 |
+| TagFeed | 10.586s | 33 |
+| Trends::Statuses | 10.295s | 12 |
+| Status | 7.861s | 62 |
+| Notification | 6.095s | 25 |
+| MediaAttachment | 5.940s | 27 |
+| PublicFeed | 5.486s | 43 |
+
+Stable fixture graphs in `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy` and `TagFeed` now use `let_it_be(..., reload: true)`. The cleanup policy's repeated relationship setup uses `before_all`. Reloads and transactional examples keep the shared records isolated while avoiding per-example reconstruction.
+
+No application code, factories, request specs, system specs, or streaming specs were changed during this phase.
+
+## Follow-up opportunities
+
+- Profile `spec/requests` independently before applying any shared job-mode changes. Request specs can rely on synchronous work that model specs do not.
+- In the factory phase, start with `account`, `status`, and `bookmark`: the models profile created each 1,194, 1,003, and 1,019 times respectively. FactoryProf nested totals overlap, so do not add them as independent runtime costs.
+- If more model gains are needed, investigate setup sharing in `Trends::Statuses`, `Status`, `Notification`, and `PublicFeed`; preserve per-example reload isolation and mutation rollback.
+- Re-run with the comparison seed above, plus at least one different seed, after changing shared setup.
